k-me-up, the afternoon slump\n3. The nighttime binger — once kids are in bed, the secret sugar session\n4. The \"healthy-ish\" sugar consumer — thinks they're moderate, but the hidden sugars add up\n5. The yo-yo dieter — cycles between strict restriction and sugar binges\n6. The social/dessert person — can't imagine celebrations without sugar\n7. The \"it's the only pleasure I have\" person — sugar as sole indulgence\n\nLet me now design focused commissions. I'll need to cover all five lanes across multiple commissions.\n\nFor the commissions, I should think about:\n- Lane A: Lived experience — multiple commissions for different personas/communities\n- Lane B: Scientific evidence \n- Lane C: Industry/villain material\n- Lane D: Counter-corpus (pro-sugar justifications)\n- Lane E: Dialect & sensory\n\nGiven the scope, I'll likely need 8-12 commissions. Let me design them carefully.\n\nLet me structure the commissions now. Each needs to be a complete, self-contained set of instructions including search patterns, output format, etc.\n\nI need to be very thorough here. Let me think about the key communities and search patterns:\n\nCommunities for sugar:\n- r/sugarfree (Reddit — but Reddit excluded, so look for mirrors, archives, or similar forums on open web)\n- Whole30 forums\n- Keto/low-carb forums\n- \"I Quit Sugar\" (Sarah Wilson) community archives\n- App reviews for quit-sugar apps, fasting apps, food tracking apps\n- \"Sugar addiction\" recovery forums\n- YouTube quit-sugar story transcripts\n- Podcast transcripts about quitting sugar\n\nScience:\n- Sugar addiction neuroscience (dopamine, reward pathways)\n- Sugar vs. cocaine studies (the famous rat studies by Lenoir et al.)\n- Metabolic effects of sugar\n- Sugar industry funding of research\n- Systematic reviews on sugar and health\n\nVillain:\n- Sugar industry internal documents\n- UCSF Sugar Science\n- Marketing to children\n- Bliss point engineering\n- The Sugar Association\n\nCounter-corpus:\n- Moderation arguments\n- \"Everything in moderation\"\n- \"Natural sugar\" defenses\n- \"Sugar is fuel\" arguments\n- \"Life is too short\" arguments\n\nLet me now write out focused commissions. I'll aim for around 10 commissions to start, covering all lanes with depth.\n\nActually, per the doctrine, I need to issue commissions for the caller to run as fresh independent research calls. Let me structure these carefully.\n\nLet me write the parameter block first, then the commissions.\n\nI think I'll do about 10 commissions:\n\nK-01: Lane A - App store reviews for sugar-cessation / food-tracking apps\nK-02: Lane A - Lived experience from sugar-free/quit-sugar community forums and blogs\nK-03: Lane A - Long-form quit-sugar stories (blogs, YouTube transcripts, podcast transcripts)\nK-04: Lane B - Scientific evidence on sugar addiction mechanisms and neuroscience\nK-05: Lane B - Scientific evidence on health consequences and prevalence\nK-06: Lane C - Industry/villain material (sugar industry documents, engineering, marketing)\nK-07: Lane D - Counter-corpus: pro-sugar justifications and willpower failure stories\nK-08: Lane E - Dialect and sensory bank for sugar\nK-09: Lane A - Additional lived experience: specific personas (nighttime binger, stress eater, social dessert person)\nK-10: Lane A - Recovery/freedom testimonies and moment-of-revelation stories\n\nWait, I should also consider that some of these could be combined more efficiently, or split differently. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nActually, the doctrine says to dispatch \"one or more fresh sub-agents per lane\" and \"the lead may split a lane across several subagents.\" So I should have multiple commissions per lane where needed, especially for Lane A (lived experience) which is the most volume-intensive.\n\nLet me reconsider. For a FULL-LENGTH book, I need:\n- ≥500 lived-experience entries\n- ≥200 graded scientific claims\n- ≥100 verbatim justifications\n- ≥50 analogy candidates\n- ≥100 dialect/sensory items\n- 5-10 long-form testimonial candidates\n\nThat's a lot. I'll need many commissions to mine all of this.\n\nLet me think about the structure more carefully and design commissions that are specific enough to yield quality material, and broad enough to generate volume.\n\nFor Lane A, I'll split by community/source type:\n- A1: App store reviews (quit-sugar apps, fasting apps, food trackers)\n- A2: Sugar-free forums and online communities (non-Reddit)\n- A3: Long-form personal blogs about quitting sugar\n- A4: YouTube/podcast quit-sugar transcripts\n- A5: Specific personas and moments (nighttime binging, office treats, celebrations)\n\nFor Lane B:\n- B1: Addiction/neuroscience mechanisms\n- B2: Health consequences\n- B3: Prevalence and demographic data\n\nFor Lane C:\n- C1: Industry documents and history\n- C2: Product engineering (bliss point, food design)\n- C3: Marketing practices\n\nFor Lane D:\n- D1: Pro-sugar justifications\n- D2: Willpower failure stories\n\nFor Lane E:\n- E1: Dialect and sensory mining\n\nThat could be about 13 commissions, which seems reasonable.\n\nBut wait, each commission needs to be self-contained and produce actual output in the correct packet + raw-bank line format. Let me write them up now.\n\nLet me be very concrete with search patterns and output formats for each commission.\n\nOK, I'll start writing. Let me produce the parameter block and commissions now.\n\nOne important note: Reddit is excluded without authorization, but I can still use subreddit names as leads to find mirrored content on other platforms (like removeddit archives, or discussions that migrated to other forums). The doctrine says \"subreddit names as leads only — Reddit itself excluded without authorization.\" So I'll instruct subagents to use Reddit as a discovery tool (finding community names, topics, slang) but to pull actual quotes from non-Reddit sources.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"Reddit is excluded without explicit Reddit authorization; browser stealth is neve